    "narrative": "The offensive outlook for LawDog today centers on **Cal Raleigh**, who stands as our clear anchor with a 74.56 efficiency score. Facing a soft matchup against Martin P\u00e9rez (4.73 xERA), **Cal Raleigh** benefits from a high-leverage spot in the two-hole, providing optimal conditions for both counting stats and power production. While we are navigating a difficult slate of opposing arms, **Tyler Freeman** presents our most intriguing \"Strong\" play at 59.49. His score is bolstered significantly by a 14% park factor boost and a favorable stolen base environment; despite facing a high-caliber arm in Freddy Peralta, the combination of a slow delivery and an elite path to the bases makes **Tyler Freeman** a critical component for category density today.\n\nElsewhere, the optimizer had to be surgical regarding platoon splits. **Lawrence Butler** is a high-upside start today because his 13% platoon edge against a right-handed pitcher offsets the brutal matchup against Zack Wheeler, with wind blowing out providing just enough variance to keep him in the starting nine. Conversely, **Gavin Sheets** remains in the lineup despite a poor historical BvP because his raw power ceiling against Houser (4.60 xERA) provides a higher floor than our depth chart alternatives. On the bench, **Daulton Varsho** was the primary candidate for a spot start, but he was ultimately relegated due to a combination of a negative platoon split against Shane McClanahan and his current status outside the active lineup, making him a non-factor for today\u2019s cap management. \n\nWe are exercising caution with several middle-tier starters today, specifically **Geraldo Perdomo** and **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez**, as both must contend with elite-level pitching in Paul Skenes and Cole Ragans, respectively. While **Jos\u00e9 Ram\u00edrez** is an untouchable cornerstone, his score is suppressed by Skenes' dominance; similarly, **Geraldo Perdomo** is only rostered to chase counting stats at 